# Citation metadata for ColabSeqDisplay, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# GitHub renders this file as the repository's "Cite this repository" entry.
#
# Five PLACEHOLDER values must be replaced before this repository is cited in a
# publication. Grep this file for PLACEHOLDER to find every one of them:
#   1. the author entry            — real authors and affiliations, in citation order
#   2. `doi:`                      — the concept DOI minted when the release is archived
#   3. `identifiers:` doi value    — the same concept DOI, again
#   4. `date-released:`            — the deposit date; uncomment it at the same time
#   5. the `references:` entry     — the real citation for the seqdisplay-opt study,
#                                    whose method this software runs
#
# In that order: list the authors, publish a tagged release and archive it in a
# DOI-issuing repository (archiving a GitHub release on Zenodo mints a DOI of the
# shape below), then paste the concept DOI into both fields and set the date.
#
# `version:` must stay equal to the version in pyproject.toml and colabsd/__init__.py.
# `license:` must stay equal to the licence in LICENSE and pyproject.toml.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use ColabSeqDisplay in your research, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
title: "ColabSeqDisplay: no-code protein language model fine-tuning for sequence-display variant libraries"
abstract: >-
  ColabSeqDisplay is a pair of Google Colab notebooks that fine-tune a protein
  language model on a sequence-display variant library and predict activity for new
  variants. Hyperparameters are looked up for the chosen backbone from a committed
  registry rather than tuned in the notebook, the test partition is locked until it is
  deliberately unlocked, and the workflow reports a one-hot baseline beside the
  fine-tuned result.
type: software
version: 0.1.0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLACEHOLDER — replace before publication.
# One entry per author, in the order they should appear in the citation.
# Delete the placeholder entry once the real authors are listed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
authors:
  - family-names: "PLACEHOLDER-FAMILY-NAME"
    given-names: "PLACEHOLDER-GIVEN-NAMES"
    affiliation: "PLACEHOLDER-AFFILIATION"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # uncomment and fill in

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PLACEHOLDER — replace with the DOI minted when this repository is archived.
# Custom code central to a study's conclusions is expected to be deposited in a
# DOI-issuing repository; archiving this GitHub repository on Zenodo mints a DOI
# of this shape. Zenodo mints two: use the *concept* DOI, which always resolves
# to the newest version, rather than the DOI of one specific release.
# Update this field, the matching identifier below, and `date-released`, at the
# moment the archive is created.
# The value below is a syntactically valid DOI, so validators pass on this file
# as it stands; it does not resolve, and is not a citable identifier.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.0000000"

identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.0000000"
    description: "PLACEHOLDER — concept DOI for all versions of the archived software."
  - type: url
    value: "https://github.com/JasonJiangs/ColabSeqDisplay"
    description: "Source repository."

repository-code: "https://github.com/JasonJiangs/ColabSeqDisplay"
url: "https://github.com/JasonJiangs/ColabSeqDisplay"
license: MIT

# PLACEHOLDER — set to the archive date (YYYY-MM-DD) when the DOI is minted.
# date-released: 2026-01-01

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The study this software is a front end for. `colabsd/engine/` is copied from
# its code and every tuned hyperparameter in `config/best/` was selected by it,
# so anyone citing ColabSeqDisplay should cite it too; listing it here is what
# makes a citation manager surface both. See ATTRIBUTION.md.
#
# PLACEHOLDER — replace `authors`, and add `doi:`/`year:`/`journal:` (changing
# `type:` to `article`), with the real citation when that study is published.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
references:
  - type: software
    title: "SequenceDisplay-Workflow-Optimization: LoRA fine-tuning and representation comparison for sequence-display activity prediction (Python package seqdisplay_opt)"
    abbreviation: "seqdisplay-opt"
    authors:
      - name: "PLACEHOLDER — the authors of SequenceDisplay-Workflow-Optimization"
    repository-code: "https://github.com/JasonJiangs/SequenceDisplay-Workflow-Optimization"
    url: "https://github.com/JasonJiangs/SequenceDisplay-Workflow-Optimization"
    notes: >-
      The method ColabSeqDisplay runs: the LoRA implementation, the training loop, the
      pooling strategies, the metrics, the head registry, the split protocol, and the
      tuned hyperparameters in config/best/. Every performance figure reported by
      ColabSeqDisplay is a measurement from this study.

keywords:
  - protein language model
  - sequence display
  - variant effect prediction
  - deep mutational scanning
  - LoRA
  - low-rank adaptation
  - fine-tuning
  - Google Colab
  - protein engineering
